Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: advance stack arguments past a retained return address

The misaligned-return path keeps the x86 return address on the physical
stack to maintain native stack alignment. The entry thunk still needs x4
to point past that address, as in the normal path. Advance x4 without
changing sp or the existing return trampoline.

Independently reproduced with a six-argument native ARM64EC helper:
aligned calls pass, while misaligned calls read argument five from the
preceding slot and argument six from argument five's slot. The probe
changes only its own caller alignment and records every argument.

Related upstream report: https://github.com/FEX-Emu/FEX/issues/6015
No game-specific code or behavior is changed.

diff --git a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.S
@@ -94,6 +94,7 @@
   br x17
 
 ret_sp_misaligned:
+  add x4, x4, #0x8 // Entry thunk stack arguments start after the x86 return address.
   // In the case of the x64 caller leaving sp only 8-byte aligned, leave the return address on the stack to keep 16-byte
   // alignment and have the callee return to an x86 ret instruction. FEX can then return to the actual caller keeping
   // the misaligned RSP.
